= Book Matter =

'''Book Matter''' is BITwiki's semantic content anatomy: recurring kinds of material inside a knowledge object that can be recognized, retrieved, composed, and—when useful—transcluded independently.

The design goal is not arbitrary fragmentation. It is to make knowledge '''transposable, modular, and composable while preserving depth and nuance.'''

== Why Book Matter exists ==
A reader or agent does not always need an entire article. One task may need only a definition. Another may require theoretical foundations, applications, historical development, controversy, or citations. Book Matter gives those portions stable semantic identities.

This supports progressive disclosure:
<pre>
orientation
→ intermediate conceptual structure
→ advanced technical depth
→ evidence / source layer
</pre>

== Historical matter vocabulary ==

=== Overview / orientation ===
* '''Definition''' — explanation / explication
* '''Summary''' — abstract
* '''Scope & importance''' — significance
* '''Historical context''' — origins, background, legacy
* '''Epistemic foundations''' — principles, premises, fundamentals
* '''Knowledge evolution''' — trajectory, development, expansion

=== Concepts ===
* Foundations
* Key ideas
* Symbolic systems
* Cross-disciplinary literacy
* Related fields

=== Frameworks ===
* Theoretical models
* Knowledge frameworks

=== Applications ===
* Case studies
* Applied knowledge
* Dynamic knowledge trends
* Expert opinions
* Interpretations / paradigms

=== Opinions / disputes ===
* Debates
* Controversies
* Competing interpretations

=== Timelines / chrono-literacy ===
* Key events / breakthroughs
* Scientific developments / discoveries
* Social and economic impact
* Future predictions
* Evolving knowledge patterns — adaptations, transformations, shifts

=== Citations & bibliography ===
* Primary sources
* Secondary sources
* Academic references
* Knowledge maps — schemas, diagrams, networks
* Historical writings — records, documents
* Open data repositories — public archives and research banks
* Contested theories / disputes

== Other recurring matter ==
Methods; mechanism; evidence/results; implementation; limitations/challenges; criticism; related concepts; see also; further reading.

== Book Matter is not taxonomy ==
A page section called ''Applications'' is not automatically a Category:Applications. A domain such as Biology is not Book Matter. An entity type such as Project is not Book Matter. An epistemic state such as Hypothetical is not Book Matter. A MediaWiki namespace is not Book Matter.

== Transclusion ==
Historical designs often represented matter as article subpages:
<pre>
{{:Bitcoin/Overview}}
{{:Bitcoin/Concepts}}
{{:Bitcoin/Frameworks}}
{{:Bitcoin/Applications}}
{{:Bitcoin/Citations}}
</pre>

That pattern remains valid where an independently addressable portion is genuinely reusable. V2 does not require every heading to become a subpage.

== Portals, indices, outlines, contents ==
The broader Book Matter research also treated the reading interface as part of knowledge architecture: portals, indices, outlines, contents, and disambiguation. These are navigational forms, not substitutes for ontology.

== Source lineage ==
Historical sources include the BITwiki Book Matter research project, ''book matter prompt'', ''BITWIKI WIKI-Philosophy'', V1 transclusion/template pages, and the live Main Page's explicit contrast between static monolith articles and '''fluid modularized content'''.

